 Deployment helpers for the internal services.
 
-`bin/notify-deploy.sh SERVICE VERSION` restarts the service on the deploy host and posts a
-message to the release channel. The webhook URL comes from `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` in the
-environment; per-service settings live in `etc/services.conf`.
+`bin/notify-deploy.sh [--dry-run] SERVICE VERSION [NOTE]` restarts the service on the deploy host
+and posts a message to the release channel. The webhook URL comes from `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` in the
+environment; per-service settings live in `etc/services.conf`. The script needs `jq`, `ssh`,
+and `curl`.
+
+## Release note
+
+An optional third argument is a short release note, up to 500 characters. It is added on a new
+line under the "Deployed SERVICE VERSION by USER" message in Slack:
+
+```sh
+bin/notify-deploy.sh web 1.4.2 'Fixes the login timeout; needs a cache flush afterwards'
+```
+
+Quote the note so the shell passes it as one argument. It is only ever sent as message text.
+
+## Dry run
+
+`--dry-run` prints what the script would do and changes nothing: it does not run `ssh` (so the
+service is not restarted and nothing is deployed) and does not post to Slack. It prints the remote
+command and the Slack payload, and reports whether `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` is set without showing its
+value.
+
+```sh
+bin/notify-deploy.sh --dry-run web 1.4.2 'Fixes the login timeout'
+```
+
+## Input rules
+
+`SERVICE` must match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$` and `VERSION` must match
+`^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._+-]{0,63}$`; anything else is rejected with exit status 65. Usage errors
+exit with 64.
